# Frontend Guidelines

## General UI Principles
- Use shadcn/ui and Tailwind CSS for all standard UI components.
- Maintain a minimalist, accessible, and responsive design.
- Follow atomic design principles for component structure (Atoms, Molecules, Organisms).
- All interactive elements must be keyboard accessible and use semantic HTML/ARIA.

## Three.js & 3D Animations

### When to Use
- Use Three.js for all 3D visualizations, advanced animations, or interactive effects in the app.
- For simple UI animations, prefer CSS or Framer Motion.

### Integration Approach
- **Preferred:** Use [React Three Fiber](mdc:https:/docs.pmnd.rs/react-three-fiber) (`@react-three/fiber`) as the React renderer for Three.js. This allows you to build 3D scenes declaratively as React components, leveraging React’s state and lifecycle[4][5].
- **Install:**  
- `three`: Core Three.js library
- `@react-three/fiber`: React renderer for Three.js
- `@react-three/drei`: Useful helpers and abstractions for common 3D tasks


- Place all 3D/Three.js components in `src/components/three/`.
- Each 3D scene or effect should be a self-contained React component.
- Use `<Canvas>` from `@react-three/fiber` as the root for any 3D scene.
- Compose 3D objects as child components of `<Canvas>`, using hooks like `useFrame` for animation[4][5].


- Import and use `<CrazyAnimation />` in your main UI as needed.

### Best Practices
- Keep Three.js logic isolated from standard UI code for maintainability[1].
- Use React hooks (`useFrame`, `useRef`, `useState`) to control animation and interactivity[5].
- For complex scenes, split objects and effects into smaller components.
- Use `@react-three/drei` for lighting, controls, and helpers to speed up development.
- For performance, avoid unnecessary re-renders and use memoization for static objects.

### Accessibility & Responsiveness
- Ensure 3D canvases are responsive (use CSS or parent sizing).
- Provide fallback UI or descriptions for users who cannot view 3D content.

